Chapter: DtlsHandshake
  cites Foreword chapter Hmac
  cites Foreword chapter Hkdf
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The DTLS 1.3 handshake machine (RFC 9147 sections 5.1, 5.7, 5.8, 7).
 Phase D2 of the protocol stack plan.

 What it is not: it does not authenticate anybody. The credential
 question (PSK vs raw public key vs X.509) is still deliberately open,
 and the flight machine is agnostic to it -- it sequences flights,
 retransmits them, and runs the cookie exchange. Whether the peer's
 certificate is acceptable is a verdict handed in from outside. Do not
 read a completed handshake here as a trusted peer.

 We say:

Section: Constants

 RFC 9147 section 5.8.2: implementations SHOULD use an initial timer
 value of 1000 ms and double the value at each retransmission, up to no
 less than 60 seconds.

  dtls-hs-initial-timer : Integer = 1000
  dtls-hs-max-timer : Integer = 60000

 The RFC does not bound the number of retransmissions -- it expects a
 human or a profile to decide when to give up. Bare metal cannot leave
 that open: an unbounded retry loop against a black hole is a hang, and
 a hang on a device nobody can reach is the failure this project exists
 to prevent. So the machine gives up after a fixed count and says so.
 This is OUR bound, not the RFC's, and it is stated rather than buried.

  dtls-hs-max-retries : Integer = 10

 RFC 9147 section 5.1: if a server receives a ClientHello with an
 invalid cookie, it MUST terminate the handshake with an
 illegal_parameter alert. That alert is 47.

  dtls-alert-illegal-parameter : Integer = 47
  dtls-alert-timeout : Integer = 80

 Flight numbers, RFC 9147 section 5.7 figure 7. Odd flights are the
 client's.

  dtls-flight-ch1 : Integer = 1
  dtls-flight-hrr : Integer = 2
  dtls-flight-ch2 : Integer = 3
  dtls-flight-fin : Integer = 5

Section: Types

  DtlsHsPhase =
    | PhStart
    | PhWaitServerHello
    | PhWaitAck
    | PhConnected
    | PhFailed

 `ActSendFlight` carries a FLIGHT identifier, not bytes, which is why it
 is named for one. It was `ActSend`, and `Net chapter Tcp` declares an
 `ActSend (List Integer)` of its own that really does carry bytes: two
 constructors, one name, different payload types, in a foreword chapter
 that is globally in scope and an OS chapter that is not. Dormant only
 while no program cites both transports -- co-cite them and it is a
 duplicate-constructor error at best and the wrong arm at worst.

  DtlsAction =
    | ActSendFlight (Integer)
    | ActSetTimer (Integer)
    | ActDeliverKeys
    | ActAck
    | ActAbort (Integer)

  DtlsEvent =
    | EvStart (Integer)
    | EvHelloRetry (List Integer) (Integer)
    | EvServerFlight (Integer)
    | EvAck (Integer)
    | EvTimeout (Integer)

  DtlsHsState = record {
    hs-phase : DtlsHsPhase,
    hs-flight : Integer,
    hs-timer : Integer,
    hs-retries : Integer,
    hs-cookie : List Integer
  }

  DtlsHsStep = record {
    step-state : DtlsHsState,
    step-actions : List DtlsAction
  }

  dtls-hs-new : DtlsHsState
  dtls-hs-new =
    DtlsHsState { hs-phase = PhStart, hs-flight = 0, hs-timer = dtls-hs-initial-timer, hs-retries = 0, hs-cookie = [] }

Section: The Step

 One event in, one state and an action list out. Every branch is total;
 an event that makes no sense in the current phase produces no actions
 and no state change, because a datagram protocol receives nonsense all
 the time and crashing on it is the attack.

  dtls-hs-step : DtlsHsState, DtlsEvent -> DtlsHsStep
  dtls-hs-step (st) (ev) =
    when ev
      is EvStart (now) -> dtls-hs-on-start st now
      is EvHelloRetry (cookie) (now) -> dtls-hs-on-hrr st cookie now
      is EvServerFlight (now) -> dtls-hs-on-server-flight st now
      is EvAck (now) -> dtls-hs-on-ack st now
      is EvTimeout (now) -> dtls-hs-on-timeout st now

 Flight 1: the bare ClientHello, no cookie. A fresh flight always
 resets the retransmission timer to its initial value -- the backoff
 belongs to a flight, not to the connection.

  dtls-hs-on-start : DtlsHsState, Integer -> DtlsHsStep
  dtls-hs-on-start (st) (now) =
    when st.hs-phase
      is PhStart -> dtls-hs-send st dtls-flight-ch1 PhWaitServerHello now []
      is otherwise -> dtls-hs-idle st

 Flight 3: the ClientHello again, this time echoing the server's cookie.
 The client does not get to decide whether to do this -- if the server
 asked, it asks because it will not allocate state until the cookie comes
 back, and a client that ignores the HelloRetryRequest simply never gets
 a handshake.

  dtls-hs-on-hrr : DtlsHsState, List Integer, Integer -> DtlsHsStep
  dtls-hs-on-hrr (st) (cookie) (now) =
    when st.hs-phase
      is PhWaitServerHello -> dtls-hs-send st dtls-flight-ch2 PhWaitServerHello now cookie
      is otherwise -> dtls-hs-idle st

  dtls-hs-on-server-flight : DtlsHsState, Integer -> DtlsHsStep
  dtls-hs-on-server-flight (st) (now) =
    when st.hs-phase
      is PhWaitServerHello -> dtls-hs-finish-flight st now
      is otherwise -> dtls-hs-idle st

  dtls-hs-finish-flight : DtlsHsState, Integer -> DtlsHsStep
  dtls-hs-finish-flight (st) (now) =
    let next = DtlsHsState { hs-phase = PhWaitAck, hs-flight = dtls-flight-fin, hs-timer = dtls-hs-initial-timer, hs-retries = 0, hs-cookie = st.hs-cookie }
    in DtlsHsStep { step-state = next, step-actions = [ActDeliverKeys, ActSendFlight dtls-flight-fin, ActSetTimer (now + dtls-hs-initial-timer)] }

 An ACK for the last flight ends the handshake. RFC 9147 section 5.8.1:
 the WAITING state is left on an ACK as well as on a timer or a peer
 flight, and this is the one that means success.

  dtls-hs-on-ack : DtlsHsState, Integer -> DtlsHsStep
  dtls-hs-on-ack (st) (now) =
    when st.hs-phase
      is PhWaitAck -> DtlsHsStep { step-state = __record-set st "hs-phase" PhConnected, step-actions = [] }
      is otherwise -> dtls-hs-idle st

Section: Retransmission

 A connected machine ignores timers. A failed one stays failed. Neither
 is an accident: a stale timer arriving after the handshake completed is
 normal on a lossy link, and re-arming on it would keep a finished
 connection retransmitting into a peer that has moved on.

  dtls-hs-on-timeout : DtlsHsState, Integer -> DtlsHsStep
  dtls-hs-on-timeout (st) (now) =
    when st.hs-phase
      is PhWaitServerHello -> dtls-hs-retry st now
      is PhWaitAck -> dtls-hs-retry st now
      is otherwise -> dtls-hs-idle st

  dtls-hs-retry : DtlsHsState, Integer -> DtlsHsStep
  dtls-hs-retry (st) (now) =
    if st.hs-retries + 1 > dtls-hs-max-retries then dtls-hs-give-up st
    else dtls-hs-retry-now st now

  dtls-hs-give-up : DtlsHsState -> DtlsHsStep
  dtls-hs-give-up (st) =
    DtlsHsStep { step-state = __record-set st "hs-phase" PhFailed, step-actions = [ActAbort dtls-alert-timeout] }

  dtls-hs-retry-now : DtlsHsState, Integer -> DtlsHsStep
  dtls-hs-retry-now (st) (now) =
    let t = dtls-hs-backoff (st.hs-timer)
    in let next = DtlsHsState { hs-phase = st.hs-phase, hs-flight = st.hs-flight, hs-timer = t, hs-retries = st.hs-retries + 1, hs-cookie = st.hs-cookie }
    in DtlsHsStep { step-state = next, step-actions = [ActSendFlight (st.hs-flight), ActSetTimer (now + t)] }

  dtls-hs-backoff : Integer -> Integer
  dtls-hs-backoff (t) =
    let doubled = t * 2
    in if doubled > dtls-hs-max-timer then dtls-hs-max-timer else doubled

Section: Step Helpers

  dtls-hs-send : DtlsHsState, Integer, DtlsHsPhase, Integer, List Integer -> DtlsHsStep
  dtls-hs-send (st) (flight) (phase) (now) (cookie) =
    let next = DtlsHsState { hs-phase = phase, hs-flight = flight, hs-timer = dtls-hs-initial-timer, hs-retries = 0, hs-cookie = cookie }
    in DtlsHsStep { step-state = next, step-actions = [ActSendFlight flight, ActSetTimer (now + dtls-hs-initial-timer)] }

  dtls-hs-idle : DtlsHsState -> DtlsHsStep
  dtls-hs-idle (st) = DtlsHsStep { step-state = st, step-actions = [] }

Section: The Stateless Cookie

 RFC 9147 section 5.1. The point of the cookie is that the server keeps
 NOTHING between the two ClientHellos: it derives the cookie from the
 client's address under a secret only it knows, and on the second
 ClientHello it recomputes and compares. An attacker who forges a source
 address never receives the cookie, so it can never produce the second
 ClientHello, so it can never make the server allocate anything.

 This is the whole denial-of-service story, and it works only because
 there is no table here to fill. If you find yourself adding a map from
 address to cookie, you have rebuilt the vulnerability.

  dtls-cookie : List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-cookie (secret) (client-addr) = hkdf-words-to-bytes (hmac-sha256 secret client-addr)

 The comparison is constant-time. A cookie check that returns early on
 the first differing byte leaks, under timing, how much of a guess was
 right -- which turns an unforgeable token into a guessable one.

  dtls-cookie-valid : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  dtls-cookie-valid (secret) (client-addr) (offered) =
    dtls-bytes-equal (dtls-cookie secret client-addr) offered

  dtls-bytes-equal : List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  dtls-bytes-equal (a) (b) =
    if list-length a /= list-length b then False
    else dtls-bytes-equal-loop a b 0 (list-length a) 0

  dtls-bytes-equal-loop : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  dtls-bytes-equal-loop (a) (b) (i) (n) (diff) =
    if i >= n then diff == 0
    else dtls-bytes-equal-loop a b (i + 1) n (bit-or diff (bit-xor (list-at a i) (list-at b i)))

 The server's verdict on an incoming ClientHello. Three outcomes and no
 fourth: no cookie offered means send a HelloRetryRequest and keep no
 state; a cookie that verifies means proceed; a cookie that does not
 verify MUST abort with illegal_parameter (RFC 9147 section 5.1) -- it
 is NOT the same as no cookie, and quietly re-issuing a
 HelloRetryRequest instead would hand an attacker an oracle and a free
 retry.

  dtls-server-verdict : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> DtlsAction
  dtls-server-verdict (secret) (client-addr) (offered) =
    if list-length offered == 0 then ActSendFlight dtls-flight-hrr
    else if dtls-cookie-valid secret client-addr offered then ActDeliverKeys
    else ActAbort dtls-alert-illegal-parameter

Section: Anti-Amplification

 RFC 9147 section 5.1: a server SHOULD limit the amount of data it sends
 toward a client address to three times the amount of data sent by the
 client, before it verifies that the client can receive data at that
 address.

 Without this, DTLS is a reflector: an attacker spoofs a victim's source
 address, sends a small ClientHello, and the server sprays a much larger
 flight at the victim. The cookie stops state exhaustion; this stops the
 bandwidth amplification. They are different attacks and both need
 closing.

  dtls-amplification-ok : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  dtls-amplification-ok (received) (already-sent) (want) =
    already-sent + want <= received * 3

  dtls-amplification-budget : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dtls-amplification-budget (received) (already-sent) =
    let left = received * 3 - already-sent
    in if left < 0 then 0 else left
